+
+## Touch and input sweep
+
+Is every control easy to touch, does every tap answer, does the screen stay where the person is looking, is typing
+understood, and does Back bring them back? The owner asked (2026-11-11) for "every single touch and input" to be
+stress tested, "focusing on easy to know next step, to go back, to navigate and to focus correctly while understanding".
+This is the machine half of that, kept in the repo so a later change cannot quietly make the app harder to use.
+
+**What it checks, in plain words.** On a 390 x 844 phone with a finger, for six invented households (first open with
+nothing typed, a family, a retired widow who rents, a young renter with debts, a freelancer, a couple near retirement),
+on every page a household is offered, and on every screen of a page that moves between screens with a button
+(Get started's questions, `#/overview/setup/spend`: `screens` in `qa/touch-rules.json`):
+
+| Rule | It breaks when |
+|---|---|
+| T-TARGET | something to tap is smaller than 44 x 44 px |
+| T-NAME | a button has no words, a box has only a placeholder for a label, or a label is tied to no box |
+| T-COVERED | something is drawn over a control, it hides under the top bar, or it runs off the side of the screen |
+| T-KEYBOARD | a box that asks for a number brings up letters, or its text is under 16 px (an iPhone zooms in) |
+| T-ONE-WAY | two filled buttons share a screen, or a page does not end with exactly one way on (design log 2026-11-06) |
+| T-LINK | a link opens a page that is missing or switched off, or its words do not name the page it opens ("Open the page" names none) |
+| T-HEAD | the top bar's title is not the menu's name, two pages share a name, or headings skip a level |
+| T-CANCEL | a form that a tap opened (+ Add another, Edit) has no Cancel, Close or Back: the only way out is to save it (a form that was already open and is only drawn again is not one) |
+| T-WIDE | the page scrolls sideways |
+| D-ANSWERS | a tap changes nothing a person can see within a second, or a filled form's button empties the form and saves nothing |
+| D-TIME | a tap (or opening a page) holds the screen for more than 400 ms of work, or takes over 2 seconds with no word that it is working (from 200 ms and 1 second it is listed, not counted: a busy computer moves a page across that line) |
+| D-FOCUS-KEPT | you type in one box, tap the next and type: the cursor or the typing is gone after the page redrew |
+| D-SCROLL-KEPT | after a save, an add, a remove or opening a fold, the control slides from under the finger or the screen jumps away from the result |
+| D-SEEN | a save, add or remove shows no confirmation inside the screen |
+| D-BACK | the phone's Back does not return to the same page, the same place on it (within 100 px) and the same open section, or the page itself scrolls to the top while Back draws it again |
+| D-ENTER | Enter in a one-line box does nothing, or something else (not a date box: a phone's picker has no Enter) |
+| D-TWICE | two fast taps on Add, or on a filled form's Save, make two rows |
+| D-KEYBOARD-UP | with the keyboard up, the box is covered (a bar pinned to the bottom rides up with the keyboard) or its form's button is more than two short screens below |
+| D-TOP | a new page does not start at the top with its title |
+| D-WRONG | a wrong value ("abc" in a money box, a number past the box's limit, nothing where needed) is taken without words on the screen, or the form loses what was typed (a box that refuses letters as they are typed has answered; the year 0026 in a date box is listed, not counted: a phone's picker cannot make it) |
+| D-KIND | "1,200", "$1,200.50", " 300 " or "5%" is refused, dropped (saved as nothing or 0) or read as another number |
+| D-UNDO | a remove neither asks first nor offers Undo |
+| D-ERROR | a tap or typing raises a JavaScript error |
+
+`qa/touch-rules.json` holds each rule in exact terms, the line it must stay on, **where the line comes from and the
+day the source was read** (a line with no published source says "ours"), and ten more rules a machine cannot hold
+a page to (`byHandOnly`: the real keyboard, zoom, thumb reach, motion, wording), each with how to check it by hand.
+
+**How to run it.**
+
+```
+node qa/touch-sweep.mjs --fast                 # 20 pages x 2 households and the menu: before a push (2.5 to 3.5 min)
+node qa/touch-sweep.mjs                        # everything: 150 pages x the six households and the menu (about 1.5 h)
+node qa/touch-sweep.mjs --pages cashflow/income,debt/payoff --households family --rules D-BACK,T-TARGET
+node qa/touch-sweep.mjs --json out.json --shots shots/touch      # every break, with a picture of the screen
+node qa/touch-static.mjs --list T-LINK         # the part with no browser, and every break of one rule
+```
+
+It needs Playwright (not a dependency of this repo: it says so and stops when it is missing; `PLAYWRIGHT=<path>` points
+at a copy). It starts the app's own server itself on a free port, **from a copy of the app in a temporary folder**, so
+`planner.properties` and `data/` here are never read or written; the app runs with `?sandbox=1`, on the pretend plan of
+each household, and the clock starts at noon on the day in `touch-rules.json` (`day`), so counts do not drift with the
+calendar. `--workers N` sets how many browsers run at once (3).
+`--every` taps every one of a long list of like controls: by default every control is measured, but of a list of more
+than eight like controls (the 157 words of the glossary, the rows of a long table) the first six and the last two are
+tapped, and of more than three like lists on a page the first two and the last; the rest are listed under "Not
+checked". With only `T-` rules named in `--rules` (other than T-LINK and T-CANCEL, which follow links and open
+forms) nothing is tapped: each page is measured as it opens and with every fold open, and a form that a tap opens is
+not reached. The pages a household is not offered (the builder's, and the ones that need the computer or the AI
+helper) are not visited. If the browser goes away under the
+sweep (a crash, a machine short of memory), it is launched again and the pages it was on are run once more from the
+start, with nothing of the lost run counted; the report says how many.
+
+**How it works.** Each control is acted on from a fresh copy of the plan, so one action never spoils the next: the
+plan is put back through the app's own store, the page is drawn anew, and the sweep checks that it reads as it did at
+the start (a page that remembers a choice of its own is loaded again). A control inside a fold is reached with only
+its own folds open; a form that a tap opens (Add, Edit, a row) is explored once per kind. Links are tapped, then Back
+and Forward; buttons are tapped, and a form's button also with the form filled in; every box is tapped, typed into,
+left for the next box, sent with Enter, and given wrong values and the four ways people type amounts. What a tap did
+is read from the screen a person sees (the words and controls inside it, the address, the plan, the browser's own
+"fill this in" messages); a tap that seems to do nothing is confirmed with two screenshots before it counts. Timings
+that cross a line are measured three more times, one at a time, in processor time, and the smallest counts.
+
+**How to read it.** The table lists, per rule: households x pages reached, things checked, breaks, the worst pages.
+"Breaks by kind" names what went wrong. "Not checked" says what was not acted on and why (a link out of the app, a
+date picker, a spared neighbour in a long list). The JSON (`--json`) has every break: rule, household, page, state
+("as it opens", "with every fold open", "after tapping ..."), the control's kind and name, where it is (`at`), what was
+measured, and a screenshot path with `--shots`; `said` holds the words the app answered each wrong value with, to read
+for sense (rule H-WORDS). A break is one control failing one rule in one state; the same root cause (the shell scrolls
+to the top on Back) shows as many breaks, and the worst pages tell where to look. Same tree, same counts: every tap
+waits until its click has reached the page before the screen is read, and Back is judged by what the page does (it
+scrolls itself to the top while Back draws it again), not by what the browser does a moment later. (Before these two,
+the counts hung on the machine's load: one `--fast` run found 74 breaks on a quiet machine and 322 on a busy one,
+2026-11-11.) Only D-TIME is a measurement: it counts from twice its line, and lists what lies between the line and
+twice it (H-SPEED says how to time those on the phone). Times above are
+from a 4-core machine shared with other work: the fast spread took 2 min 26 s to 2 min 49 s when it was quiet and up to
+3 min 31 s when other browsers ran beside it; the whole sweep took 85 minutes beside them.
+
+**When to run it.** `--fast` before moving `main` after any change to a `view.js`, to `core/app.js`, `core/ui/` or
+`modules/overview/guide/`; the whole sweep after a change to the shell or the UI kit, and before a release. `npm test`
+always runs the part that needs no browser (`tests/touch-rules.test.mjs`, about 15 seconds: names, labels, number
+keyboards, links and their words, labels tied to no box, one filled button per card or form, the way on, headings, a
+way to close an opened form) on every page for the six households, on Get started's questions and on every guide
+journey a page links to, drawn with the repo's fake DOM (`qa/fixtures/fake-dom.mjs`) as each page
+opens and once more for each "+ Add", Edit or Write button on it, tapped (`qa/touch-static.mjs`).
+
+**How we know it catches things.** On 2026-11-11 a "breaker" seeded about thirty defects into a copy of the app, one at
+a time (a 30 px button, a label tied to nothing, a link renamed "Open ›", a Save that throws the form away, a fixed bar
+over the keyboard, Back that forgets the place, ...), ran this sweep on each, judged forty of its breaks against
+screenshots, and changed the sweep wherever it missed a defect or counted something that was not one; each change
+says so in its rule's `check` ("Breaker, 2026-11-11"). After a change to the sweep itself, seed one defect of the
+rule you touched the same way and see it caught, on the right page and control.
+
+**How KNOWN works.** `tests/touch-rules.test.mjs` holds `KNOWN`, the count of breaks per rule on the day it was
+written. A rule with breaks is `todo` while its count stays at or under KNOWN, so the suite is green; one more break
+fails it, naming the page and the control; a count that falls prints a line asking to lower KNOWN, so it cannot creep
+back. Never raise a KNOWN count to make a change pass: fix the page, or, when the break is right by design, say so
+in `touch-rules.json` (an exception with its reason) in the same change.
+
+**How to add a rule.** Add it to `qa/touch-rules.json` (id `T-` for a static rule, `D-` for one that needs an action;
+concern; the check in exact terms; the threshold; its source with an address, and the day you read it, or "ours";
+`where`: browser, node or both). Check it in `qa/touch-sweep.mjs` (static rules in `statics`, what a tap did in
+`judge`, a form a tap opened in `run`), and, when it needs no screen, in `qa/touch-static.mjs` (`judgePage`, or
+`runStatic` for a part a tap opens) with its count in KNOWN; a judge both halves share goes in `qa/touch-lib.mjs`. The
+test fails until a rule in the JSON is checked by the sweep and a "both" rule is counted in the test. A new kind of
+control or a new shell behaviour (a fixed bar, a sheet, a second scroll area) needs its rule in the same change
+(docs/CONVENTIONS.md "Touch and input").
