+   "check": "For every tap that stays on the page, and for opening each page: (a) the page's main thread does no more than 200 ms of work from the tap until the page is settled; (b) the page is settled within 1 s, or words on the screen say it is working ('Working', 'Calculating', 'Loading', 'One moment', an aria-busy or a progress element). A tap is first timed on the clock (its event duration, the figure INP is built from, and its long tasks); one that crosses a line is taken three more times, one at a time, with the work measured in processor time (Chromium's TaskDuration in thread ticks, so a busy machine does not count), and the smallest of the three decides. A link's tap is not timed: the page it opens is, once, as its own opening. No CPU slow-down is applied: the figures are this machine's, a floor for a phone. Counted from twice the line (400 ms of work; 2 s to settle with no word); between the line and twice it, listed as near the line, not counted: the same page measured 190 ms on a quiet machine and 271 to 316 ms beside other browsers, processor time and all, so the count hung on the load (Breaker, 2026-11-11: two --fast runs on the same tree differed only there). Time the listed ones on a phone by hand (H-SPEED).",
